---
# yamllint disable rule:line-length
# Widens the item local of a keep-label frame that a capture peel has written
# over. Every frame in the 503/504/510 family names local 1 from the distill's
# `bridge-input`, on the standing assumption that slot 1 still holds the item
# the wrapper was handed. A capturing operator breaks it: 316 (and 314 for the
# capturing filter) park the item mid-body with `astore 1` — its own, by then
# dead, parameter slot — so that the captures can be fetched off the state list
# in front of it. What sits in slot 1 from there on is the item AT THAT POINT
# of the chain, which is only `bridge-input` when nothing ahead of the peel
# retyped it.
#
# A retyping head ahead of a capture peel is what makes them disagree:
#
#   Stream.of(1, 2, 3).map(n -> n / 2.0).map(d -> d * frac)
#     .peek(d -> ...).filter(d -> d > 1.0)
#
# fuses into one stateful distill whose `bridge-input` is Ljava/lang/Integer;,
# whose head retypes the item to a Double, and whose capture peel then parks
# that Double in slot 1. The keep-label frame the filter needs still promised
# the Integer, and the class died at link time with "Inconsistent stackmap
# frames ... Type 'java/lang/Double' (current frame, locals[1]) is not
# assignable to 'java/lang/Integer' (stack map, locals[1])". It was latent for
# as long as nothing could retype the head of a captured run; 208 made
# `IntStream.chars().peek(...).mapToObj(n -> n / 2.0).map(d -> d * frac)
# .filter(...)` — seed 53 of RandomPipeline — one of the shapes that can.
#
# `java/lang/Object` is what the frame is widened to, rather than the parked
# type spelled out, because the merge does not need more than that and nothing
# reads slot 1 expecting a narrower one: the peels are the only writers of that
# slot and each of them stores before it loads, while 512's own `aload 1` is
# the first opcode of the body, ahead of every frame. The item on the STACK is
# untouched — 503/504/506 name it from the operator that consumes it, and that
# is the binding those rules exist for.
#
# It runs at fixpoint behind every frame writer in the family (the 510 number
# puts it behind the relayed pair as well, whichever of them writes first) and
# the `having` below makes it idempotent, so a frame it has already widened is
# not rewritten into itself forever.
name: distill-parked-frame
pattern: |
  ⟦
    φ ↦ Φ.hone.distill-lambda,
    static ↦ 𝑒-static,
    bridge-input ↦ 𝑒-bridge-input,
    consumer ↦ 𝑒-consumer,
    target-method ↦ 𝑒-target-method,
    captured ↦ 𝑒-captured,
    body ↦ ⟦
      𝐵-body-head,
      𝜏-park ↦ ⟦ φ ↦ Φ.jeo.opcode.astore, i1 ↦ 1 ⟧,
      𝐵-body-mid,
      𝜏-frame ↦ ⟦
        φ ↦ Φ.jeo.frame,
        type ↦ 𝑒-frame-type,
        locals ↦ ⟦
          φ ↦ Φ.jeo.seq.of4,
          x0 ↦ 𝑒-x0,
          x1 ↦ 𝑒-x1,
          x2 ↦ 𝑒-x2,
          x3 ↦ 𝑒-x3
        ⟧,
        stack ↦ 𝑒-frame-stack
      ⟧,
      𝐵-body-tail,
      ρ ↦ ∅
    ⟧
  ⟧
result: |
  ⟦
    φ ↦ Φ.hone.distill-lambda,
    static ↦ 𝑒-static,
    bridge-input ↦ 𝑒-bridge-input,
    consumer ↦ 𝑒-consumer,
    target-method ↦ 𝑒-target-method,
    captured ↦ 𝑒-captured,
    body ↦ ⟦
      𝐵-body-head,
      𝜏-park ↦ ⟦ φ ↦ Φ.jeo.opcode.astore, i1 ↦ 1 ⟧,
      𝐵-body-mid,
      𝜏-frame ↦ ⟦
        φ ↦ Φ.jeo.frame,
        type ↦ 𝑒-frame-type,
        locals ↦ ⟦
          φ ↦ Φ.jeo.seq.of4,
          x0 ↦ 𝑒-x0,
          x1 ↦ "java/lang/Object",
          x2 ↦ 𝑒-x2,
          x3 ↦ 𝑒-x3
        ⟧,
        stack ↦ 𝑒-frame-stack
      ⟧,
      𝐵-body-tail,
      ρ ↦ ∅
    ⟧
  ⟧
having:
  not:
    eq:
      - 𝑒-x1
      - '"java/lang/Object"'
